{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310866,"nonce":"oVeQYUKtg6BbaGu1aWaxeM1wI4g=","title":"Converting rayzor.ds.Tensor + QTensor from `extern class` to `@:coreType extern abstract` is a VERIFIED BEHAVIORAL NO-OP — identical ...","message":"User direction (from [[bugs_simd4f_broken_two_part]]) was \"Tensor/QTensor become @:coreType abstracts (zero method-call overhead)\". Investigated exhaustively 2026-06-29; **RESOLVED = keep `extern class`**, the conversion buys nothing.\n\nChanged both to `@:coreType ... extern abstract` (kept @:derive([Clone]) @:shared @:native). Ran the SAME tests on both forms; **every result identical**:\n- Full haxe suite: 155 (extern) / 156 (abstract — the +1 is a test added this session, not an abstract effect), 0 crashed, 0 W0020, q4km QTensor tests PASS — both.\n- nue's real `Null\u003cTensor\u003e` bias pattern (`if (bias != null) out = out.add(bias)` — Null\u003cT\u003e as ARGUMENT): WORKS both (1→2→done).\n- `Null\u003cT\u003e` as method RECEIVER (`if (b != null) b.get()/.add()`): CRASHES both, same exit codes (132/132/138-139).\n- nue tiny-transformer (cross-module): SIGILL exit 132 at the same point (\"smoke test\" header) — both.\n\n**Why no payoff:** Tensor/QTensor methods are all `@:native` FFI calls into Rust kernels. `@:coreType` abstract changes the *type modeling* (value-type vs class) but NOT the runtime representation (both are opaque pointers to the same Rust struct) and NOT the call (the kernel IS the call). So \"zero method-call overhead\" never materialises. Codegen treats both forms as opaque handles — hence byte-identical behavior.\n\n**Decision:** kept the known-good `extern class` (what nue/llama-chat run on today). The abstract is safe to apply anytime for type-modeling reasons, but delivers no measurable benefit. Could matter LATER if Tensor methods ever get Haxe bodies (self-hosting) — not now. Couldn't run llama-chat as the final gate (no GGUF model on disk), but 156 suite + all Null patterns + tiny-transformer being byte-identical makes a production-only divergence very unlikely.\n\nThe right lever is NOT converting Tensor to @:coreType (it's a heap struct, can't be a register value, and the conversion would mis-mark it Send/Sync — see below). The lever is **give the method a Haxe body** (drop @:native), using the @:coreType SIMD primitives for the inner math. The compiler inlines Haxe bodies; it CANNOT inline @:native methods (they're `body:None`/is_extern, no CFG — inlining.rs:201-204).\n- **In-tree precedent that already does this:** `gpu/Vec4.hx:23` — `add(other){return new Vec4(x+other.x,...)}` is a pure-Haxe body, NO FFI. The door is open; nobody walked Tensor through it.\n- **What makes SIMD ops inline (NOT the annotation):** (1) hardcoded name-detection in convert_type() hir_to_mir.rs:22337 `if is(\"SIMD4f\"){return IrType::vector(F32,4)}` (other @:coreType abstracts Float/Int/Ptr→I64/I32, NOT vector); (2) a register-sized (128-bit) value → operators lower to a single VectorBinOp (hir_to_mir.rs:19584), not a call. SIMD4f method intercept at hir_to_mir.rs:12927.\n- **Tensor stays Ptr(Void) and that's CORRECT:** RayzorTensor is a heap struct (data*,shape*,strides*,dtype,refcount(Atomic),parent*) — runtime/src/tensor.rs:555-599. Refcounted views (@:shared, parent chains, AcqRel refcount) need stable heap identity. Haxe gets the raw data via `data():Ptr\u003cFloat\u003e` (Tensor.hx:433) — the cursor to loop over with SIMD.\n- **Spectrum of de-FFI (all available now):** L0 (now) Haxe `add`→wrapper `Tensor_add`→extern `rayzor_tensor_add` (2 hops, runtime_mapping.rs:3562 + stdlib/tensor.rs:955 build_binop_i64 macro). L1 mark the wrapper `InlineHint::Always` (Tensor_matmulT ALREADY is — stdlib/tensor.rs:1048) → 1 hop, still FFI, trivial. L2 Haxe body over SIMD4f+Ptr (Vec4 pattern) → no FFI, inlinable = the real migration.\n- **Why the conversion is mildly WRONG for Tensor (not just inert):** verifier refuted \"changes nothing\" — extern class (TypeKind::Class) → @:coreType abstract (TypeKind::Abstract, None underlying) makes trait_checker.rs:208-227 treat it Send/Sync like a primitive, bypassing the @:shared-governed thread-safety of a refcounted heap struct.\n- **The two gates to shipping Haxe kernels (neither is \"can't\"):** (1) wasm SIMD-wrapper inlining = THE lever ([[perf_haxe_qmatmul_blocked_on_simd_inlining]], 28×/90× until inner SIMD calls inline on wasm — stdlib merged post-inliner). (2) hand-tuned Rust kernels (matmul ~500 LOC, SDOT, threading) at llama.cpp parity; pure-Haxe at-best-parity → latency says keep hot ops FFI, portability/self-hosting says move them.\n\n1. **Method call on a `Null\u003cTensor\u003e`/`Null\u003cQTensor\u003e` RECEIVER crashes** (single-file `rayzor run`): `var b:Null\u003cTensor\u003e = Tensor.ones(..); if (b != null) b.get([0]);` → SIGILL 132 (and `.add` on a Null receiver → SIGSEGV 138/139). Crashes on BOTH extern class and abstract → not the conversion. Repro /tmp/n1.hx, /tmp/ta.hx, /tmp/tc.hx. NOTE the apparent contradiction: nue's Linear.forward/Embedding.forward use exactly `if (qweight != null) qweight.matmulXTQThreaded(..)` (method on Null receiver) and llama-chat RUNS — so either cross-module Null-narrowing differs from single-file, or it's method-specific. Null\u003cT\u003e as an ARGUMENT is fine; only as a method receiver crashes. Worth a dedicated fix (nue-relevant pattern).\n2. **nue tiny-transformer (F32 path) SIGILL exit 132** right after the \"smoke test\" header — both forms, no panic/W0020/trap diagnostic, plain SIGILL. Pre-existing F32-transformer-path bug, separate from the quantized llama-chat path (which runs). Needs libnue_plugins.dylib built first (`cargo build --release -p nue-plugins`) or you get \"can't resolve symbol alloc\" (missing native-lib, not a code bug).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_tensor_coretype_abstract_noop` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}